15 0 obj
<< /A 41 0 R /Alt (Flowchart detailing a study of 618 patients with Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus from July 2023 to April 2025. Data was collected via pulse wave analysis and clinical indicators, with missing values addressed through simple imputation. After excluding patients due to tumors, cognitive impairments, and incomplete data, 485 participants remained: 242 in the diabetic foot group and 243 in the control group. Using LASSO and logistic regression, six predictors were identified. A predictive nomogram model was constructed and validated using bootstrap resampling with one thousand iterations, assessing ROC, calibration curve, and DCA.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 31 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
16 0 obj
<< /A 42 0 R /Alt (Graph depicting height \(h\) in millimeters versus time \(t\) in seconds. Peaks and troughs are labeled as H1, H3, H4, and H5 indicating height variations. Time intervals are marked as T1, T4, T5, and total duration T. Widths W1 and W2 are indicated near the first peak.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 32 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
17 0 obj
<< /A 43 0 R /Alt (Plot showing binomial deviance versus log-transformed lambda values. Red dots represent data points with error bars. Two dashed vertical lines at approximately -5.3 and -2.9 mark specific values, with deviance increasing beyond these points.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 35 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
18 0 obj
<< /A 44 0 R /Alt (Plot showing coefficients in a LASSO regression against the logarithm of Lambda. Each colored line represents different coefficients, converging near zero as Lambda increases. Dashed vertical lines likely indicate specific values of interest for Lambda.) /K 1 /P 8 0 R /Pg 35 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
19 0 obj
<< /A 45 0 R /Alt (A medical scoring chart with multiple scales indicating points based on different variables. Variables include Gender, BMI \(Body Mass Index\), HTN \(Hypertension\), SBP \(Systolic Blood Pressure\), BUN \(Blood Urea Nitrogen\), and H5H1. Each variable has a corresponding point scale. Total points and diagnostic possibilities are shown on separate axes.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 36 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
20 0 obj
<< /A 46 0 R /Alt (ROC curve graph comparing test and train data. The x-axis shows 1 minus specificity, and the y-axis shows sensitivity. The red line represents test data with an AUC of 0.786 \(confidence interval: 0.694-0.877\). The blue line represents train data with an AUC of 0.750 \(confidence interval: 0.702-0.799\). A diagonal dashed line indicates random performance.) /K 1 /P 8 0 R /Pg 36 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
21 0 obj
<< /A 47 0 R /Alt (Two calibration plots compare actual probability versus predicted probability. Plot \(A\) shows near-perfect calibration with lines close to the ideal diagonal. Plot \(B\) deviates more, illustrating discrepancies. Both display statistical metrics like Dxy and Brier scores for model evaluation.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 37 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
22 0 obj
<< /A 48 0 R /Alt (Two decision curve graphs labeled A and B. Both graphs plot net benefit against threshold probability, ranging from 0 to 0.8, with cost-benefit ratios displayed on the x-axis. Graph A shows a decreasing red line labeled \215Nomo\216 and a gray line labeled \215All,\216 while graph B shows a similar trend but ends with an upward spike in the red line. Both graphs also include a baseline labeled \215None.") /K 1 /P 8 0 R /Pg 37 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
